// Two bars share the same 55px strip at the bottom of the screen:
//
//   [ right ]  full width, owns the exclusive zone, holds media/backlight/volume/
//              tray/battery/logout. Nothing in here ever moves.
//   [ ws    ]  drawn on top of it, with its own surface that stops well before the
//              right edge ("margin-right"), so the workspace list lives in its own
//              lane and can never push the right hand modules off screen again.
//
// A list that outgrows its lane is handled visually, not by hiding anything: the
// tail of the list is faded out in style.css so it dissolves towards the icons on
// the right, while the workspace you are on always stays fully visible - even when
// it is empty and sits at the very end of the list.
//
// Notes when changing this:
//   * Bar order matters: later bars stack on top of earlier ones, and a bar on top
//     swallows every pointer event over its own surface (waybar's "passthrough"
//     does not actually release them). "ws" must stay last so the workspace
//     buttons keep their clicks, and its surface must stay clear of the right hand
//     modules so those keep theirs.
//   * Do not set "mode": "dock" here - it moves the bar to the "bottom" layer,
//     i.e. underneath windows.
//   * Only "right" is exclusive; "ws" is pulled back down into the reserved strip
//     with "margin-bottom": -55, which must match "height".
//   * WIDTH RESERVED FOR THE RIGHT MODULES = the margin-right value below (900).
//     That is the worst case width of the right hand group (~660px idle, ~780px
//     with a track playing, ~60px more while custom/reboot is showing) plus
//     slack. It keeps the "ws" surface off the right
//     hand modules so they always get their own clicks; it does not squeeze the
//     workspace list, because waybar cannot clip a bar to its own surface - the
//     "ws" surface simply grows to whatever its contents need.
//   * Because of that growth, a very long workspace list eventually draws over the
//     right hand modules rather than stopping at the lane. The fade in style.css
//     is what keeps that from looking broken - widen the fade there (or tighten the
//     button spacing) if you regularly run more workspaces than fit.
[
    {
        "name": "right",
        "layer": "top",
        "position": "bottom",
        "exclusive": true,
        "passthrough": false,
        "gtk-layer-shell": true,
        "height": 55,
        "no-center": true,
        "include": ["~/.config/waybar/modules.jsonc"],
        "modules-left": [],
        "modules-right": ["custom/media","backlight","pulseaudio","pulseaudio#microphone","tray","custom/reboot","battery","custom/wlogout"]
    },

    {
        "name": "ws",
        "layer": "top",
        "position": "bottom",
        "exclusive": false,
        "passthrough": false,
        "gtk-layer-shell": true,
        "height": 55,
        "margin-bottom": -55,
        "margin-right": 900,
        "no-center": true,
        "include": ["~/.config/waybar/modules.jsonc"],
        "modules-left": ["clock", "custom/weather", "hyprland/workspaces"],
        "modules-right": []
    }
]
